 for plane polar coordinates states that\n\n$$\n\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,dA=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D\\left(3 x^{2} + 2 x y - 3 y^{2} + 3 y\\right)\\,dA&=\\iint_\\Delta\\left(- 3 r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 2 r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 3 r^{2} \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 3 r \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right)r\\,dr\\,d\\theta\\\\&=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{3}r^{2} \\left(r \\sin{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)} + 3 r \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)} + 3 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right)\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186519811 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519790 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186519811 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186519811 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/44/44c489cbdf4665cca7fc7b8a331f5ccf4d72f8d15577f58c45497c58d5817bf3.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ shown above. Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral\n\n$$\n\\oint_C(x y^{2} + 4 y^{3} - y \\cos{\\left(x \\right)})\\,dx+(4 x^{3} + 2 x^{2} y + 2 x y - \\sin{\\left(x \\right)})\\,dy\\,?\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186519811 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "87c025ce-7c7a-5364-86fe-6c2155437fe3" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186519811 #edb/ref 1074 "Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint_C P\\,dx+Q\\,dy=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA.\n$$\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/c4/c4cf3906e5af81f6cb071bae5b594861351987934fe6d51443c09e17982b2204.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=x y^{2} + 4 y^{3} - y \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}$ and $Q(x,y)=4 x^{3} + 2 x^{2} y + 2 x y - \\sin{\\left(x \\right)},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=2 x y + 12 y^{2} - \\cos{\\left(x \\right)},\\qquad\\frac